<p>Sharding divides ownership of data across database servers. One server owns some rows, another owns others, and a request must find the right owner or combine work from several. The point is to distribute storage or useful work that one server can no longer carry within the service’s goals.</p>

<p>The division has a cost. A join that used to read two local tables may now exchange rows over the network. A purchase may need several owners to agree before it can commit. A failed or slow owner can hold up a result assembled from all of them. Choosing the boundary determines how often those costs enter ordinary requests.</p>

<p>We extend the shop to serve two tenants: shop 10, the original shop, and shop 20, Harbour Tea. A tenant here is a shop, not one of its customers. This tenant-scoped variant makes a useful candidate boundary because many requests concern one shop’s orders, lines and stock. The placement drawings are explanatory designs, not observations of a running distributed database.</p>

<h2 id="three-ways-to-divide-the-problem">Three ways to divide the problem</h2>

<p><em>Table partitioning</em> divides one logical table into pieces, often by date or another key. PostgreSQL’s native partitions remain tables managed by the same database service. Pruning old partitions from a query or dropping an old month can reduce work and simplify lifecycle management. Creating those partitions does not by itself spread ownership across independent servers.</p>

<p><em>Replication</em> keeps copies of the same data. A replica can provide another place to read or a copy to promote after a failure, subject to its replication and routing guarantees. Replicating every write to another server does not divide those writes between independent owners. It adds copies and work, even when reads can be spread.</p>

<p><em>Sharding</em> gives different subsets different owners. Those subsets can each have replicas. “Three shards, each with three replicas” describes nine copies of shard data, not nine independent copies of the complete database. Every shard still needs a write protocol and enough surviving copies.</p>

