<p>First establish which pressure you have. Query work, leaked connections or one long transaction can exhaust a server without requiring distribution. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/connections-and-capacity/">Connections, overload and capacity</a> supplies the evidence and headroom; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/partitioning/">Table partitioning and data lifecycle</a> and <a href="/learn/databases/relational/replicas-and-read-routing/">Replicas, lag and read routing</a> develop the other two mechanisms.</p>

<h2 id="carry-the-boundary-in-the-data">Carry the boundary in the data</h2>

<p>Shop 10’s P7 is the Blue mug at 20.00. Shop 20 can have its own P7, the Copper mug at 18.00. In this variant a product’s identity is <code>(shop_id, product_id)</code>. An order’s is <code>(shop_id, order_id)</code>, and each child reference carries that same shop ID.</p>

<Sql label="The tenant boundary in a reference" code={`-- Tenant-scoped variant, abbreviated to the relevant keys:
products:    PRIMARY KEY (shop_id, product_id)
orders:      PRIMARY KEY (shop_id, order_id)
order_items: PRIMARY KEY (shop_id, order_id, line_no)

-- On order_items:
FOREIGN KEY (shop_id, order_id)
  REFERENCES orders (shop_id, order_id)
FOREIGN KEY (shop_id, product_id)
  REFERENCES products (shop_id, product_id)`} />

<p>A line for shop 10 must refer to shop 10’s order and product. Carrying the boundary into the reference preserves meaning even when the same short ID appears elsewhere. It also supplies a value a router and query planner can use. It does not authorise a caller to read a shop: access control remains a separate check.</p>

<p>For the next drawing we choose a placement policy: put each shop’s related rows together and record which shard owns that shop. Read order 103’s receipt, then ask for P7 without naming a shop.</p>

<Routing />

<p>The receipt is local because its order, lines and products share the chosen boundary. The P7 lookup lacks that boundary and must inspect both owners in this scheme. It also returns two legitimate products: the missing shop is a question about identity as well as routing.</p>

<p>A complete tenant key does not automatically co-locate tables. An application that maintains separate shard databases must route every related table consistently. Citus has distribution-column and co-location configuration. CockroachDB distributes its ordered key space into ranges and replicates those ranges; a table or tenant can occupy many ranges. Treat the drawing as a candidate locality design, then inspect how the selected system actually implements it.</p>

<h2 id="choose-the-key-from-the-work">Choose the key from the work</h2>

<p>A <em>shard key</em> supplies the information used to assign a row to an owner. It is useful when ordinary requests know it and related work shares it. Shop ID is strong for per-shop order history, stock changes and reports. It is weak for a global search that knows only product text or an order lookup whose public ID does not reveal its shop.</p>

<p>List the important operations and the facts each must read or change. For the shop’s purchase, those are product stock, the order, its lines and any atomic request-outcome record. Under tenant placement they can be local to one shop. A promotion voucher limit shared across all shops is outside that boundary. Calling it a tenant operation would not make the rule local.</p>

<p>Then ask how load follows the key. Ten thousand similarly busy shops can spread well. Ten thousand shops with one responsible for most writes may leave one owner saturated. Hashing shop ID distributes different shops; it does not divide the hot shop’s single hash value.</p>

<p>A finer boundary, such as order ID, can spread one shop’s order history. It can also separate an order from the stock it changes, and make a per-shop list contact several owners. A product boundary keeps one product’s stock together but separates an order containing P7 and P3. The gain and the new cross-boundary operations come from the same choice.</p>

<p>Count bytes and work as well as rows. Equal row counts do not imply equal storage if one tenant stores large values. Equal storage does not imply equal request load. A successful design accounts for the largest tenant, hottest key, write pattern, expensive query and failure-state demand, rather than only the average tenant.</p>

<h2 id="hashes-ranges-and-growing-keys">Hashes, ranges and growing keys</h2>

<p>Two common mappings make different requests cheap. A hash mapping turns a key into a bucket, tending to spread many distinct values. A range mapping puts neighbouring ordered values together. The first helps distribute independent keys; the second lets a bounded range query visit neighbouring pieces.</p>

