<p>In an ordered distributed index, a monotonically increasing leading key can send new inserts to the current end range. Splitting old data does not spread writes that all move to the new end. A hash prefix can change that pattern, but a time-range query then has several ordered runs to read. CockroachDB’s hash-sharded indexes make this trade explicitly.</p>

<p>Do not copy that advice unaltered into a single-server B-tree. Sequential keys can give dense pages and cheap inserts there. The new concern is ownership of the changing end across servers, rather than merely how full one page becomes.</p>

<p>A hot single key is different again. Hashing that same key always sends it to the same bucket. Splitting a range can separate different hot keys, but cannot divide one row’s decision among independent owners while preserving its original semantics. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/rebalancing/">Rebalancing and cluster change</a> follows the distinction between moving load and removing its source.</p>

<h2 id="what-a-global-query-must-do">What a global query must do</h2>

<p>A query without a useful routing condition may <em>fan out</em>: ask several shards, then combine their replies. Some work can stay local. To count all qualifying orders, each shard can count its own and send one number. To join two tables distributed on unrelated keys, the system may need to move many rows until matching values meet.</p>

<p>Global ordering also needs a global answer. Asking each of three shards for its ten newest orders gives up to thirty candidates; returning all thirty as the “newest ten” is wrong. A coordinator must compare candidates under one total order and choose ten. Distributed execution can make this efficient, but it does not remove the semantic step.</p>

<p>Fan-out latency depends on the participating work and exchanges. A slow shard can delay the whole result, and the coordinator needs memory and processing capacity to combine it. Adding shards can reduce work per shard while increasing messages and the number of places that can be slow. Keep per-shard work, rows exchanged and coordinator work in the plan and measurement.</p>

<p>Secondary indexes deserve the same map. An index ordered by product name may place entries differently from the primary rows. A lookup can first find an index entry, then contact the primary row’s owner. An update may write both owners. A covering index can save that lookup at the cost of more stored and written data. “The row is local” does not yet account for all its index work.</p>

<h2 id="who-coordinates-the-change">Who coordinates the change?</h2>

<p>With application-managed shards, the application or its routing service knows the map and owns operations that cross databases. A local foreign key cannot enforce a reference to a separate ordinary database. A local commit cannot atomically commit another shard. You must either keep the invariant local, add a suitable cross-shard protocol or choose a business process with explicit intermediate states.</p>

<p><em>Distributed SQL</em> moves much of that routing, execution and transaction coordination into one database system with a SQL interface. The system can present tables and transactions across storage owners. This is an architectural category, not one identical promise: SQL coverage, isolation, constraints, failure behaviour and operational limits differ.</p>

<p>The abstraction is valuable because an application can express one transaction without implementing each message. The messages still exist. Replicas must agree about their portion; participants must agree about the transaction; readers need a coherent view. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/distributed-transactions/">Transactions across partitions</a> follows the commit boundary, while <a href="/learn/databases/relational/distributed-reads/">Time, snapshots and distributed reads</a> follows the read boundary.</p>

<p>A successful connection to a nearby SQL node does not prove the rows or their write authority are nearby. A local transaction can still replicate to distant voters. Keep three maps distinct: where a request enters, where the operation is coordinated and where durable copies live.</p>

<h2 id="make-the-distribution-decision-testable">Make the distribution decision testable</h2>

<p>Start with a statement of the limiting resource and service goal on one server. Map representative operations to proposed owners, including their indexes and invariants. Estimate how much stays local, what fans out, what coordinates, and which single tenant or key can defeat balance.</p>

<p>Build a production-shaped sample and inspect actual placement and plans. Test local requests, global queries, a hot tenant and concurrent writes to a shared rule. Record engine version, distribution configuration, data generation, row sizes, network topology, offered load and useful completions. A design diagram cannot establish measured latency or automatic rebalancing behaviour.</p>

<p>Include movement and failure in the trial. How does a router learn a new owner? Can an old owner still accept a write? What happens to retries while a shard moves? How long do backups, restores and schema changes take across the layout? The steady-state speedup must leave enough resource to repair and change the cluster.</p>

<p>Distribution is justified when the independent work it creates pays for the coordination and operation it introduces. Sometimes the correct next step is a larger server, fewer repeated queries or replicas for suitable reads. Sometimes a tenant boundary gives many independent units with little cross-shard work. Deriving the boundary from the workload makes that judgement reviewable.</p>

